  "title": "Filter first, then map",
  "slides": [
    {
      "type": "title",
      "text": "Only some items? filter() 🧹",
      "say": "Want to show only some items of an array? Filter first, then map."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "code": "const chemists = people.filter(p =>\n  p.profession === \"chemist\"\n);",
      "highlight": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "say": "Filter keeps only the items where your test returns true. Here, only the chemists."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "code": "const listItems = chemists.map(p =>\n  <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>\n);\n\nreturn <ul>{listItems}</ul>;",
      "highlight": [
        1
      ],
      "say": "Then map that smaller array to list items, and render them."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "filter makes a new array. map turns it into JSX.",
      "say": "Takeaway: filter picks the items, map turns them into JSX. Both create new arrays."
    }
  ]
}
